'''zerda''' (zėr′dä̤), ''n.'' A small African fox; a fennec. {{smaller|The name is applied to two very different animals: (''a'') ''Vulpes'' or ''Fennecus zerda,'' a small true fox. See ''fox''<sup>1</sup>, and cut under ''fennec.'' (''b'') ''Otocyon'' or ''Megalotis lalandi.'' See ''Megalotinæ.''}}

'''zereba, zeriba,''' ''n.'' See ''zareba.''

'''Zerene''' (zē-rē′nē), ''n.'' [NL. (Hübner, 1816; Treitschke, 1825), prop. ''Xerene,'' < Gr. ξηραίνειν, dry up.] A notable genus of geometrid moths, typical of a family ''Zerenidæ'' or subfamily ''Zereninæ.'' {{smaller|They have broad, entire, and slightly hyaline wings; the body is slender, and the male antennæ are plumose, with the branches long, slender, and slightly frizzled. The most noted species is ''Z. catinaria'' of the northern United States, a white moth, often with blackish dots, whose greenish-yellow black-spotted larva feeds on a variety of forest-plants.}}

'''Zerenidæ''' (zē-ren′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL. (Guenée, 1844), < ''Zerene'' + ''-idæ.''] A family of geometrid moths, comprising many beautiful forms, usually white or yellow, spotted with black. {{smaller|It includes 20 genera, of which ''Abraxas'' is the most important. From their maculation they are known as ''panther-'', ''jaguar-'', or ''magpie-moths,'' and one genus is called ''Pantherodes.''}}

'''Zereninæ''' (zer-ē-nī′nē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Zerene'' + ''-inæ.''] The ''Zerenidæ'' as a subfamily of ''Geometridæ.''

